  "theory": {
"intro": "An exponential with base $a > 0$ and $a \\ne 1$ is integrated by dividing by the natural logarithm of its base: $\\int a^{x}\\,dx = \\dfrac{a^{x}}{\\ln a} + C$. Differentiating $a^{x}$ multiplies by $\\ln a$, so integrating divides by it.",
"facts": [
"$\\int a^{x}\\,dx = \\dfrac{a^{x}}{\\ln a} + C$ for a constant base $a > 0$ with $a \\ne 1$.",
"Differentiating $a^{x}$ multiplies by $\\ln a$, so integrating $a^{x}$ divides by $\\ln a$.",
"Multiplying by $\\ln a$ instead of dividing is the most common error.",
"$\\int a^{mx+c}\\,dx = \\dfrac{a^{mx+c}}{m\\ln a} + C$ for $a > 0$, $a \\ne 1$ and $m \\ne 0$, the chain rule contributing the extra divisor $m$. When $m = 0$, the integrand is the constant $a^{c}$, so its integral is $a^{c}x + C$. When $a = 1$, the integrand is $1$, so its integral is $x + C$.",
"When $a = e$ the rule collapses to $\\int e^{x}\\,dx = e^{x} + C$, because $\\ln e = 1$.",
"$\\ln a$ is a fixed number, so $\\dfrac{1}{\\ln a}$ behaves like any other constant factor.",
"The power rule does not apply here, because $x$ is the index and $a$ is the base.",
"Since $\\ln a^{k} = k\\ln a$, rewriting the base gives the same primitive in a different form.",
"For a definite integral, evaluate $\\left[\\dfrac{a^{x}}{\\ln a}\\right]_{p}^{q}$ and leave $\\ln a$ in the exact answer.",
"Check any primitive by differentiating it back to the integrand."
],
"steps": [
"Write the integrand in the form $ka^{mx+c}$ with base $a > 0$. The logarithm formula requires $a \\ne 1$ and $m \\ne 0$; if $a = 1$ or $m = 0$, integrate the constant instead.",
"Keep the multiplier $k$ and write down $a$ to the same index, unchanged.",
"Divide by $\\ln a$, and by $m$, the coefficient of $x$ in the index, to obtain $F(x) = \\dfrac{ka^{mx+c}}{m\\ln a}$.",
"Add $+C$ for an indefinite integral, or evaluate $\\left[F(x)\\right]_{p}^{q}$ for a definite one."
],
