  {
    "question_text": "Find each indefinite integral.\n(a) $\\int 8\\times 3^{4-2x}\\,dx$\n(b) $\\int 7^{x/4}\\,dx$",
    "solution_text": "(a) The coefficient of $x$ in the index is $-2$.\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int 8\\times 3^{4-2x}\\,dx\\\\&=\\frac{8\\times 3^{4-2x}}{-2\\ln 3}+C\\\\&=-\\frac{4\\times 3^{4-2x}}{\\ln 3}+C\\end{aligned}$\n\n(b) The coefficient of $x$ in the index is $\\frac14$.\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int 7^{x/4}\\,dx\\\\&=\\frac{7^{x/4}}{\\frac14\\ln 7}+C\\\\&=\\frac{4\\times 7^{x/4}}{\\ln 7}+C\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Use the exponential integration formula with base $a=e$ to find $\\int e^{4x}\\,dx$.",
    "solution_text": "Since $\\ln e=1$,\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int e^{4x}\\,dx\\\\&=\\frac{e^{4x}}{4\\ln e}+C\\\\&=\\frac{e^{4x}}4+C\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Find $\\int(8^x-4x)\\,dx$.",
    "solution_text": "Integrate each term separately.\n$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int(8^x-4x)\\,dx\\\\&=\\frac{8^x}{\\ln 8}-\\frac{4x^2}{2}+C\\\\&=\\frac{8^x}{\\ln 8}-2x^2+C\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Evaluate $\\int_0^2 3^{x/2}\\,dx$, giving the exact value.",
    "solution_text": "$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int_0^2 3^{x/2}\\,dx\\\\&=\\left[\\frac{2\\times 3^{x/2}}{\\ln 3}\\right]_0^2\\\\&=\\frac{2\\times 3^1-2\\times 3^0}{\\ln 3}\\\\&=\\frac4{\\ln 3}\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Find the exact value of $\\int_0^2(3^x-2x)\\,dx$, then give it correct to four decimal places.",
    "solution_text": "$\\begin{aligned}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\int_0^2(3^x-2x)\\,dx\\\\&=\\left[\\frac{3^x}{\\ln 3}-x^2\\right]_0^2\\\\&=\\left(\\frac9{\\ln 3}-4\\right)-\\left(\\frac1{\\ln 3}-0\\right)\\\\&=\\frac8{\\ln 3}-4\\\\&\\approx3.2819\\end{aligned}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "A curve has gradient $\\frac{dy}{dx}=5^{x+2}$ and passes through $(-2,4)$. Find its equation and exact $y$-intercept.",
    "solution_text": "Integrating gives\n$y=\\frac{5^{x+2}}{\\ln 5}+C$.\n\nSubstitute $(-2,4)$:\n$\\begin{aligned}4&=\\frac1{\\ln 5}+C\\\\C&=4-\\frac1{\\ln 5}\\end{aligned}$\n\nTherefore,\n$y=\\frac{5^{x+2}-1}{\\ln 5}+4$.\n\nAt $x=0$,\n$\\begin{aligned}y&=\\frac{25-1}{\\ln 5}+4\\\\&=\\frac{24}{\\ln 5}+4\\end{aligned}$\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $\\left(0,\\frac{24}{\\ln 5}+4\\right)$."
  }
]
